<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ramachandra (Yadava dynasty)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. c. 1311</span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Ramachandra of Devagiri, Ramadeva</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ramachandra was the last major king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seuna-yadava-dynasty">Seuna Yadava dynasty</a>, ruling from Devagiri from 1271. In 1296 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alauddin-khalji">Alauddin Khalji</a> raided Devagiri and forced him to pay a huge tribute. After a renewed invasion by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malik-kafur">Malik Kafur</a> in 1308 he became a loyal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate</a>, and soon after his death the kingdom was annexed.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ramachandra-of-devagiri.jpg" alt="Ramachandra (Yadava dynasty)" width="320" height="164" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Classical Numismatic Group · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Seuna_(Yadava)._Ramachandra._1270-1311_Central_lotus_blossom,_two_%C5%9Bri,_conch,_and_%E2%80%9C%C5%9Bri_rama%E2%80%9D_in_Devanagari_above_standard_left,_each_in_incuse.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
